 "result": "The repo is `tessel`, a Flask-like framework. The mechanism is a tag-based JSON serializer.\n\n**Serializer:** `TypedCookieSerializer` in `src/tessel/json/tag.py:219`.\n- It keeps an ordered list of tag classes (`default_tags`, line 238) and runs a value through them in order.\n- On the way out it calls `tag()`, which wraps non-JSON types in a one-key dict such as `{\" t\": [...]}`. On the way in, `untag()` reverses that.\n\n**Tag classes**, all subclasses of `TypeMarker` (`tag.py:60`), each a different tagger:\n- `TagTuple` (`tag.py:133`): tuple ↔ JSON list.\n- `TagBytes` (`tag.py:159`): bytes ↔ base64 string.\n- `TagDateTime` (`tag.py:205`): datetime ↔ HTTP-date string.\n- `TagDict` (`tag.py:93`) escapes user dicts that happen to look like a tag.\n- `PassDict` (`tag.py:119`) and `PassList` (`tag.py:147`) recurse into plain containers.\n- The same file also has `TagMarkup` and `TagUUID`.\n\n**Session wiring:** `src/tessel/sessions.py`\n- `session_json_serializer = TypedCookieSerializer()` (line 273).\n- `SignedCookieSessionInterface` (line 284) sets `serializer = session_json_serializer` (line 300) and passes it to the signing serializer at line 316.\n- The session object is `SignedCookieSession` (line 57).\n\nSo on save, the session dict goes through `TypedCookieSerializer.tag()` into JSON and then gets signed. On load, the cookie is verified and `untag()` restores the original types.",
